# PowerShell Shell Environment — MANDATORY

## The Rule

The user's shell is **PowerShell** on Windows. ALL shell commands MUST use PowerShell-compatible syntax. **NEVER** use bash/sh/zsh syntax.

---

## Git Commits — No Heredoc

PowerShell does NOT support bash heredoc (`<<'EOF'`). Use one of these alternatives:

### Option 1: Simple double-quoted string (preferred for short messages)

```powershell
git commit -m "Short commit message here"
```

### Option 2: PowerShell backtick for line breaks (for multi-line messages)

```powershell
git commit -m "First line`n`nSecond paragraph here"
```

### Option 3: PowerShell variable for complex messages

```powershell
$msg = @"
First line

Detailed description here.
"@
git commit -m $msg
```

> **CRITICAL**: The `@"` must be the LAST thing on its opening line, and `"@` must be at the START of its closing line (column 0). PowerShell here-strings are strict about this.

---

## Other Bash-isms to Avoid

| Bash Syntax | PowerShell Equivalent |
|---|---|
| `&&` (chain commands) | `; if ($LASTEXITCODE -eq 0) {` or just use `;` |
| `\|\|` (fallback) | `; if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) {` |
| `$(command)` in strings | `$(command)` works in PS too, but beware context |
| `export VAR=val` | `$env:VAR = "val"` |
| `cat file` | `Get-Content file` or just use the Read tool |
| `grep` | `Select-String` or use the Grep tool |
| `find` | `Get-ChildItem -Recurse` or use the Glob tool |
| `rm -rf` | `Remove-Item -Recurse -Force` |
| `mkdir -p` | `New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force` |
| `> /dev/null 2>&1` | `| Out-Null` or `*> $null` |
| `echo` | `Write-Output` (or just use response text) |
| Single quotes in heredoc | Use PowerShell here-string `@'...'@` |

---

## Reminder

Before every Shell tool call, verify:

- [ ] No `<<` heredoc syntax
- [ ] No bash-only operators (`&&`, `||` as control flow)
- [ ] Paths use backslash or forward-slash (both work in PS)
- [ ] String quoting is PowerShell-compatible
